The only close call of the game happened during the quest.  After
waking up the Nemesis (Minion of Huhetotl) I proceeded to bungle this
somewhat stupidly.  The Minion of Huhetotl used the "run away! run
away!" school of combat to flee upstairs before summoning a whole bunch
of extra-nasty nasties (including a few non-Elbereth respecting 'H' and
'A' ... and some 'R').  Grimley barely managed to level-port out of
that mess (44/226 HP @ airlift time) in one piece.  Of course, he wound
up in the quest goal level, w/ no means of level teleporting out.

Trapped by the giant cluster-[censored] upstairs, it was now time to
get serious.  No more faffing about without DSM and un-enchanted AK
armour.  Wish #5 was consumed by +3 gray DSM.  That combined with a few
well-written scrolls of enchant armour left Grimley's AC in the "happy
zone" at AC:-22.  'R' and 'R' were genocided simply because of what was
lurking about upstairs.

Baiting the Minion of Huhetotl back downstairs wasn't immediate, a
Jabberwock managed to chew through this armour and knock off 82 HP
before the bait-and-retreat was accomplished.  Once downstairs, the
summon nasties routine was repeated ... ugh!

After *finally* getting the Minion and clearing out the quest end
level, it was time to perform "crowd control" upstairs.  No sense
leaving unfinished business^W^W a giant ass mess behind yourself.
Especially when that mess includes some summoning monsters.

After clearing out the quest, the rest of the game was a way-too-easy
cakewalk.  Grimley didn't have access to Ludios, so couldn't buy a
whole bunch of proctection ... it didn't really matter, though.  While
exploring the dungeon on the way to Medusa's lair, yet another magic
marker was found.  Grimley made a fourth attempt at a highly enchanted
ring of increase damage.  This time, it didn't blow up.  Proudly
sporting a +8 ring of increase damage, a hefty collection of +7
weapons, and an AC of -37, he knew he'd win any game of "Let's see who
hits harder" the dungeon denizens felt compelled to challenge him to.

Medusa kindly made herself into an historic statue.  Grimley was a bit
bummed about this, since he came prepared for an actual fight.

Between Medusa's lair and the Castle, Grimley came across the bones of
a wizard named 'T' ... the poor bastard tried to melee a Minotaur with
a +2 Magicbane.  After killing the Minotaur, Grimley assumed ownership
of Magicbane (plus some other stuff).

Upon reaching the Castle, he decided to showcase his musical talents.
The drawbridge was malfunctioning, however.  It kept going up and down,
squishing his audience.  The castle wand was (0:3), he used it on some
frivolous luxaries (spellbooks of magic mapping and remove curse).

Gehennom was a piece of cake.  After marking this wierd vibrating
square (with all his cash), it was time to go the Wizard his wake-up
call.  This prompted a wish for a ring of teleport control (he'd lost
his other one), followed by a levelport.

It would help, however, to actually WEAR the ring of teleport control
when levelporting ... woops!  After a bit of lolly-gagging in the
Castle (inventory checkup and confused charging) it was time to for the
final run.  One final look-see and wait-a-second, Grimley didn't have
any charged /digging left (or b?charging) and couldn't remember where
he'd stashed his pick-axe ... another oops!  One wish and one marker
application later, and he was good to go.

The Wizard was a complete pushover.  The recap:

T=37449 broke open tower, "Thou shalt repent of thy cunning, creiten"
T=37450 fumbled finger of death (85% failure)
	The Wizard of Yendor casts a spell! Double Trouble...
T=37454 You hit the Wizard of Yendor!  You kill the Wizard of Yendor!
T=37456 You hit the Wizard of Yendor!  You kill the Wizard of Yendor!

After performing everyone's favorite ritual, it was time to clear out
the sanctum.  Watching the replay still leaves me highly amused.  A
pair of purple foo-liches immediately appeared by the upstair.  Shortly
thereafter, their bodies crumbled to dust.  The longest it took to
destroy any monster was either 2 or 3 hits.  Two-weaponing with a +8
ring of increase damage was making everything *WAY TOO EASY*.

The run for Dlvl.1 was (somewhat) uneventful.  The mysterious force was
absurd (I stopped counting at the 10th throwback).  Grimley wound up
going through Orcus town (at least) 3 times and Baalz' lair (at least)
4 times.  Someone gated in Yeenoghu (bad demon!), but he didn't last
long enough to cause any (real) trouble.

The elemental planes weren't as difficult as I remembered.  Air was
still scary, though.  ';' were genocided during water just because.  On
Astral I went center->left->right (as always).  Unfortunately, the
correct altar was to the right.  The guardian angel spent the entire
time faffing about in the lobby, so Grimley didn't use conflict.  None
of the riders managed to get off an attack, or took more than 2 hits to
kill by melee (exploiting the movement system made them pushovers)  It
took exactly 227 turns to fight through the hordes and visit all three
altars.

The High Priest of Quetzalcoatl gave Grimley "2 bits for an ale" on the
theory that, if you're about to pull up to the demi-god bar, you may as
well have enough cash for a cold one.

